+		assert.deepStrictEqual(received, [{ jsonrpc: '2.0', id: 1, result: { items: [summary(remoteProject), summary(urlProject)] } }]);
+	});
+
 	test('passes through error and unknown responses and consumes response correlation once', () => {
 		const { underlying, transport, received } = createTransport();
 		transport.send({ jsonrpc: '2.0', id: 1, method: 'subscribe', params: { channel: session } });

From c943139badaac17ea42c24612c42d35ca0f1b36b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 13:19:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Give the directory mappers direction-named entry points

Both `_toHostMap` and `_toClientMap` satisfied the union-typed `map`
parameter, so a call site could pass the wrong direction and still compile,
which is the opposite of what the brands are for.

The mapping call sites no longer take a direction argument at all.
`_mapDirectoriesToHost`, `_mapDirectoriesToClient`, `_mapActionToHost` and
`_mapActionToClient` are the only entry points, and the shared
implementations they delegate to are the one place the union survives.
